I do not know the time; the clocks have flown,  
The platforms bloom with luggage, teeth, and rain;  
A glittering trout sings on the boat’s bow.  

The ticket gates are whispering in bone,  
My train exhales a garden down the lane;  
I do not know the time; the clocks have flown.  

A porter crowns the pigeons, one by one,  
While moons arrive in teacups from the Seine;  
A glittering trout sings on the boat’s bow.  

I run, but all my shoes are made of stone,  
Announcements melt to honey in my brain;  
I do not know the time; the clocks have flown.  

The rails uncoil like snakes toward the unknown,  
A mast grows from the engine’s windowpane;  
A glittering trout sings on the boat’s bow.  

My name departs without me, overblown,  
Late bells are falling upward through the train;  
I do not know the time; the clocks have flown,  
A glittering trout sings on the boat’s bow.
